            <ArticleTitle>Synthesis and SAR of potent and selective tetrahydropyrazinoisoquinolinone 5-HT(2C) receptor agonists.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3914-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bmcl.2013.04.061</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-894X(13)00542-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The 5-HT2C receptor has been implicated as a critical regulator of appetite. Small molecule activation of the 5-HT2C receptor has been shown to affect food intake and regulate body weight gain in rodent models and more recently in human clinical trials. Therefore, 5-HT2C is a well validated target for anti-obesity therapy. The synthesis and structure-activity relationships of a series of novel tetrahydropyrazinoisoquinolinone 5-HT2C receptor agonists are presented. Several members of this series were identified as potent 5-HT2C receptor agonists with high functional selectivity against the 5-HT2A and 5-HT2B receptors and reduced food intake in an acute rat feeding model upon oral dosing.</AbstractText>
